Subject: [PATCH] t2bce: rebuild hibernation queue graph

A hibernation image cannot assume that bridgeOS retained registrations for
BCE command, VHCI, or audio queues across S4.  Capturing live host queue
objects causes the restored kernel to reuse indices and DMA mappings from a
firmware epoch that no longer exists.

Force the hibernation freeze and poweroff callbacks through a complete
no-state path.  Remove the virtual HCD, ask bridgeOS to sleep without saved
state, and locally discard all client and core queues without sending
unregister commands.  Capture the image with the transport absent.  Thaw
and restore perform a fresh firmware handshake, rebuild command queues,
rebuild every client queue graph, and only then let VHCI re-add its HCD and
audio reconnect.

If RESTORE_NO_STATE is unavailable after real S4, use the probe-time
firmware handshake as a new epoch.  Restrict this fallback to the image
restore callback; ordinary S3 and hibernation unwind remain fail-closed.
Refuse the no-state path when any registered client lacks queue
reconstruction callbacks, and suppress client completion after a failed
rebuild.

This remains an offline diagnostic candidate.  It applies after the shared
DMA-blocking experiment, requires rebuilding t2bce_core, t2bce_vhci and
t2bce_audio together, and has not been installed or hardware-qualified.

